WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.201 --> 00:00:08.839
I came home from vacation
recently, jet-lagged, tired,

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:11.776
after delayed flights and a lost bag,

00:00:11.776 --> 00:00:13.277
with my cranky preschooler

00:00:13.310 --> 00:00:15.646
and my husband trudging behind me,

00:00:15.646 --> 00:00:17.815
and I forgot the code to my front door.

00:00:18.616 --> 00:00:21.552
We've lived in this house for seven years.

00:00:21.552 --> 00:00:24.822
I'd put the code in the door
hundreds of times before.

00:00:25.322 --> 00:00:28.826
And as I stood there,
staring at the keypad, I thought,

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:31.295
"Oh boy, I'm finally losing my mind."

00:00:32.296 --> 00:00:36.200
Our brains have an incredible
capacity to store memory,

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:39.570
but not all of that information
is accessible at once.

00:00:40.404 --> 00:00:43.040
An article in
“Scientific America[n]” estimates

00:00:43.074 --> 00:00:48.045
the human brain can store 2.5
million gigabytes of data.

00:00:48.746 --> 00:00:51.082
That's about 5,000 iPhones.

00:00:51.115 --> 00:00:53.751
But if you've ever forgotten
a password before,

00:00:53.784 --> 00:00:57.888
you know that memory
is not always easy to access

00:00:57.922 --> 00:01:00.958
because memory is not just one system.

00:01:01.459 --> 00:01:04.695
Those different systems have limits,

00:01:04.695 --> 00:01:09.166
and a number of factors in our daily lives
can impact our memory efficiency.

00:01:09.567 --> 00:01:12.002
There was a famous
neuropsychology case study

00:01:12.036 --> 00:01:14.972
of a patient referred to as patient HM,

00:01:14.972 --> 00:01:17.808
who became like a real-life Ten-Second Tom

00:01:17.842 --> 00:01:20.744
after brain surgery
to treat a seizure disorder.

00:01:21.579 --> 00:01:25.015
In terms of his seizures,
the surgery was a success.

00:01:25.316 --> 00:01:27.685
They were less severe and less frequent.

00:01:28.419 --> 00:01:30.020
But after his surgery,

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:34.058
HM became unable to consciously
learn new things.

00:01:34.592 --> 00:01:39.163
For example, he worked with the same
neuropsychologist for years.

00:01:39.196 --> 00:01:40.764
He never remembered meeting her,

00:01:40.798 --> 00:01:43.067
he’d introduce himself each time.

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.302
And he never remember
doing the different tasks

00:01:45.336 --> 00:01:47.304
that she would bring to test his memory.

00:01:48.105 --> 00:01:49.673
But here's the interesting part.

00:01:50.674 --> 00:01:54.979
His performance got better
on some of those tasks over time.

00:01:55.846 --> 00:01:57.648
He never remember doing them.

00:01:57.648 --> 00:02:00.384
He always thought he was doing
them for the first time.

00:02:00.417 --> 00:02:04.188
But his brain had learned the procedures
without him being aware.

00:02:04.722 --> 00:02:07.558
We learned from patient HM
and others like him

00:02:07.591 --> 00:02:09.793
that there are different types of memory.

00:02:10.427 --> 00:02:13.497
Some memories come to mind
almost automatically.

00:02:13.898 --> 00:02:16.967
Like if you're walking through a garden
and you smell the flowers

00:02:17.001 --> 00:02:19.470
that your grandma
used to keep on her kitchen table,

00:02:19.503 --> 00:02:23.307
and all of a sudden, you're thinking
of your favorite memory with your grandma.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.745
But other memories take more brainpower
and effort to recall.

00:02:27.745 --> 00:02:30.247
Like trying to remember
my new computer password.

00:02:31.315 --> 00:02:35.786
Because some memories take more
brainpower and effort to recall,

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:38.589
they're also more prone to interference.

00:02:39.323 --> 00:02:40.925
You're at the grocery store,

00:02:40.958 --> 00:02:44.161
and you’re trying to remember
10 things that you need to pick up.

00:02:44.161 --> 00:02:46.730
But you run into your neighbor
in the produce section,

00:02:46.764 --> 00:02:50.234
and after a few minutes of chit chat,
hearing about their new car,

00:02:50.267 --> 00:02:53.137
you can only remember two
of those 10 things.

00:02:53.938 --> 00:02:57.007
Even though our memory storage
is pretty impressive --

00:02:57.041 --> 00:02:59.643
5,000 iPhones big --

00:02:59.677 --> 00:03:03.180
the short-term memory that you're using
to keep track of your grocery list

00:03:03.214 --> 00:03:06.517
is more like seven, plus or minus
two, bits of information.

00:03:07.318 --> 00:03:12.089
And it's really easy to throw that active
short-term memory off track.

00:03:12.523 --> 00:03:13.757
The problem?

00:03:13.757 --> 00:03:17.061
We live in a very
distracting modern world.

00:03:17.528 --> 00:03:19.063
You're at a meeting at work,

00:03:19.096 --> 00:03:21.832
and you're trying to remember
all the details of a project

00:03:21.832 --> 00:03:23.033
that you're working on

00:03:23.067 --> 00:03:25.002
so that you can share it with your team.

00:03:25.035 --> 00:03:26.203
But at the same time,

00:03:26.237 --> 00:03:29.607
your brain is paying attention
to what your colleagues are saying,

00:03:29.607 --> 00:03:33.077
trying to ignore all the email alerts
popping up on your computer,

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:35.346
distracted by text messages
from your family

00:03:35.379 --> 00:03:36.981
asking what's for dinner,

00:03:37.014 --> 00:03:39.416
and wondering if they're going
to take a break soon

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:41.652
because you could really
use some more coffee.

00:03:42.753 --> 00:03:46.423
Ignoring all those distractions
and competing priorities

00:03:46.457 --> 00:03:48.359
takes up a lot of energy.

00:03:49.226 --> 00:03:51.528
When our brains are juggling 400 things,

00:03:51.562 --> 00:03:54.164
we're more likely to make a memory error,

00:03:54.164 --> 00:03:56.667
like forgetting a colleague’s
name in a meeting

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.802
or missing a key part
of your presentation,

00:03:58.836 --> 00:04:01.272
and then beating yourself up
for the rest of the day

00:04:01.305 --> 00:04:02.873
for making that mistake.

00:04:02.906 --> 00:04:05.342
Which brings us to another
brain-draining habit

00:04:05.376 --> 00:04:06.744
that can mess up your memory

00:04:06.744 --> 00:04:09.713
and make it hard to remember
where you left your keys:

00:04:09.713 --> 00:04:11.181
stress.

00:04:11.181 --> 00:04:13.817
Doesn't have to be huge
or major stressors, either.

00:04:14.451 --> 00:04:20.324
All those day-to-day stresses, pressures
and deadlines take up brain space, too,

00:04:20.357 --> 00:04:23.761
and force our brain to do
a bunch of mental multitasking.

00:04:25.195 --> 00:04:30.401
Last summer, I locked myself
in a stairwell twice in the same week

00:04:30.401 --> 00:04:33.237
because I was trying to do
this mental multitasking.

00:04:33.971 --> 00:04:35.873
I was packing up at the end of the day,

00:04:35.873 --> 00:04:37.074
and at the same time,

00:04:37.074 --> 00:04:39.977
I was thinking about phone calls
I needed to make tomorrow,

00:04:39.977 --> 00:04:42.680
and ideas for a project
that I was working on

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:45.082
and what I was going to make
for supper that night

00:04:45.115 --> 00:04:48.585
and whether I should pick up my daughter
before or after I get groceries.

00:04:48.619 --> 00:04:51.288
And I left my keys in my office.

00:04:51.322 --> 00:04:53.190
Twice in the same week.

00:04:53.490 --> 00:04:55.926
Apparently, door locks aren't my thing.

00:04:56.427 --> 00:05:01.532
An article from Queen's University
estimates we have 6,200 thoughts each day.

00:05:02.166 --> 00:05:04.568
That's a lot of potential
for distractions.

00:05:05.469 --> 00:05:07.771
There was a study
at the University of California

00:05:07.805 --> 00:05:12.276
that found when we're interrupted,
like with distractions or multitasking,

00:05:12.309 --> 00:05:14.812
we try to compensate by working faster.

00:05:15.179 --> 00:05:17.281
But this makes us feel more stressed.

00:05:17.314 --> 00:05:20.351
And as you can imagine,
it's not very efficient.

00:05:21.552 --> 00:05:24.621
We've all been in situations
where we feel a bit stressed out

00:05:24.655 --> 00:05:27.324
and overwhelmed by our
never-ending to do lists.

00:05:27.858 --> 00:05:32.663
Between emails and phone calls
and Zoom meetings and project deadlines,

00:05:32.696 --> 00:05:36.834
we’re coming home from work feeling tired,
and our brains are burnt out.

00:05:38.035 --> 00:05:41.205
There was an article published
in the journal “Science of Learning”

00:05:41.205 --> 00:05:45.309
that showed stress not only impacts
our memory retrieval,

00:05:45.342 --> 00:05:48.579
so it's hard to remember
that door code when you need it,

00:05:48.612 --> 00:05:51.749
stress also makes it hard
for our brains to problem-solve

00:05:51.782 --> 00:05:53.884
and think of creative solutions,

00:05:53.917 --> 00:05:57.821
like how I'm going to get into my house
when I can't remember my door code.

00:05:57.821 --> 00:06:01.058
We know all those day-to-day
stresses, pressures

00:06:01.091 --> 00:06:03.360
and deadlines can mess up our memory.

00:06:03.894 --> 00:06:06.363
But what about bigger stresses,

00:06:06.397 --> 00:06:10.634
like the uncertainty and change
of living through a multi-year pandemic,

00:06:10.667 --> 00:06:13.537
financial pressure, family stress?

00:06:14.071 --> 00:06:17.174
These stresses drain brain resources, too,

00:06:17.207 --> 00:06:19.476
making us more likely to make mistakes

00:06:19.476 --> 00:06:22.813
and making it harder to remember
the things we need to remember.

00:06:23.614 --> 00:06:26.383
So memory errors are normal,

00:06:26.417 --> 00:06:29.153
especially when you’re tired, overworked

00:06:29.153 --> 00:06:30.354
or stressed out.

00:06:31.054 --> 00:06:32.322
That's great to know,

00:06:32.356 --> 00:06:35.392
but I still need to get into my house
at the end of my vacation.

00:06:36.059 --> 00:06:38.562
How do we boost our memory
in these situations

00:06:38.595 --> 00:06:40.664
and recharge those brain cells?

00:06:40.998 --> 00:06:43.767
It's tempting to try
and just think harder,

00:06:43.767 --> 00:06:45.536
but that doesn't always work.

00:06:45.569 --> 00:06:49.273
Have you ever been trying to think
of the name of a movie or an actor,

00:06:49.306 --> 00:06:52.810
and it's right on the tip of your tongue,
but you can't quite remember it?

00:06:52.843 --> 00:06:55.479
And then four hours later,
you're driving home from work

00:06:55.512 --> 00:06:57.714
and that name comes to you
out of the blue?

00:06:58.248 --> 00:06:59.750
Yeah, me too.

00:06:59.783 --> 00:07:00.984
Totally normal.

00:07:02.052 --> 00:07:03.587
When it comes to our memory,

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:05.989
thinking harder doesn't always work.

00:07:06.557 --> 00:07:08.859
Because of a process
of competitive inhibition

00:07:08.892 --> 00:07:10.627
of the neurons in your brain.

00:07:11.495 --> 00:07:16.867
Basically, when you try to think too hard
about one super specific piece of memory,

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:19.102
like a name or a code,

00:07:19.102 --> 00:07:23.173
the brain cells in that part of your brain
get overloaded and blocked.

00:07:23.740 --> 00:07:26.176
They need time to recharge.

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:30.080
That’s why, four hours later,
that memory comes back to you.

00:07:30.113 --> 00:07:32.983
It's still there,
still in your memory storage,

00:07:33.016 --> 00:07:37.554
just the access route has been exhausted
and needs time to reset.

00:07:37.588 --> 00:07:41.492
Plus, remember how stress makes
our brains less efficient?

00:07:42.092 --> 00:07:44.761
When we're worried about making mistakes,

00:07:44.761 --> 00:07:47.564
we're more likely to make more mistakes.

00:07:47.598 --> 00:07:49.199
Do this instead:

00:07:49.233 --> 00:07:51.301
if you’re feeling
more forgetful than usual,

00:07:51.301 --> 00:07:52.536
or your brain feels stuck

00:07:52.569 --> 00:07:55.005
and you can't seem to find
the memory you need,

00:07:55.038 --> 00:07:57.975
it might be a sign
that your brain needs a reset.

00:07:58.942 --> 00:08:02.579
Can’t remember a code or a password,
standing at your door like me,

00:08:02.613 --> 00:08:05.082
and you can think of every
four-digit code you know,

00:08:05.115 --> 00:08:06.984
except the one you need?

00:08:06.984 --> 00:08:09.653
Think about something else
for a few minutes.

00:08:09.653 --> 00:08:10.821
Anything else.

00:08:11.288 --> 00:08:14.391
Chat with the store clerk at the grocery
store about the weather

00:08:14.391 --> 00:08:16.326
instead of staring at the debit machine,

00:08:16.360 --> 00:08:18.028
trying to remember your bank code,

00:08:18.061 --> 00:08:21.431
or pull out your phone and scroll
social media for a few minutes.

00:08:22.065 --> 00:08:24.935
This works because instead
of exhausting the brain cells

00:08:24.968 --> 00:08:26.870
you need to find that memory,

00:08:26.904 --> 00:08:29.473
you activate other parts of your brain,

00:08:29.506 --> 00:08:33.277
giving those memory centers
a chance to recharge and reset.

00:08:34.478 --> 00:08:36.747
Making more mistakes than normal at work,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:39.082
you can’t seem to send
an email without a typo

00:08:39.116 --> 00:08:42.019
or you’re stumbling
over your words in a meeting ...

00:08:42.019 --> 00:08:43.954
get up and take a real break.

00:08:44.321 --> 00:08:45.889
Go for a walk around your office

00:08:45.923 --> 00:08:48.091
or do something fun for a few minutes.

00:08:48.892 --> 00:08:52.195
These micro breaks
refresh your mental resources

00:08:52.229 --> 00:08:55.632
and give your brain back
the energy and focus it needs

00:08:55.666 --> 00:08:57.301
to find that information.

00:08:58.569 --> 00:09:01.638
Memory errors aren't a sign
you're losing your mind.

00:09:01.672 --> 00:09:05.275
They might be a sign your brain
needs a little break and a reset.

00:09:05.742 --> 00:09:06.910
Thank you.

